so I need to warn you fair warning I am  new to this practice of talking about  social policy that's because until  recently I was engaged in implementing  social policy as the Commissioner of New  York City's largest public social  services agency I was responsible for  the day-to-day operation of some of our  nation's biggest anti-poverty programs  at the New York City human resources  administration we administered  everything from cash welfare to food  stamp benefits to Medicaid eligibility  to child support collections and a host  of other programs in between so my  perspective comes from someone who's  seen these programs work day to day over  a long period of time and what I learned  and what I saw was that government can  create an environment whereby people in  poverty can move in to work and raise  their incomes but it takes adherence to  certain core principles and those  principles are we must require work in  return for assistance we must also  reward work by making low wages go  farther we must always talk about  two-parent families and the importance  of mothers and fathers in the  appropriate raising of children and  finally we must be vigilant about  promoting a job producing growing  economy low wage jobs middle wage jobs  highway jobs providing opportunity to  low income Americans now I suppose it's  possible that you might be thinking how  did I how did someone like me get into  the poverty fighting world to begin with  I mean  look at me frankly I look kind of  Republican even even conservative and I  am but I chose to make my career helping  struggling Americans because of what I  saw in my father's career working in the  Civil Rights Division of the United  States Justice Department he came to  Washington in 1960 at the end of the  eyes our administration and joined the  Civil Rights Division at that point and  stayed through President Kennedy's  administration and almost to the end of  the Johnson administration and during  that time he was engaged or involved in  all of the really difficult problems and  conflicts that occurred during that time  and it was positive and forward-looking  and it made our country better and  ultimately it was inspiring because it  showed that government and us Americans  could improve our country and then at  the request of his friend Robert Kennedy  we moved to New York City where Robert  Kennedy had started a small anti-poverty  program in bedford-stuyvesant one of the  largest slums in America at the time a  neighborhood of 500,000 New Yorkers in  the central Brooklyn and my father was  involved in a small program bringing  economic development and and private  sector investment and some real estate  development and over his experience  there they made some small gains for a  few people on a few blocks in Brooklyn  but meanwhile larger forces were at work  larger forces were at work and they were  the welfare entitlement state that had  said as part of the reforms that took  place in the 60s and 70s we will provide  assistance to people without regard to  what they do as a matter of right or a  matter of entitlement so while a few  people were benefited by the  bedford-stuyvesant Restoration  Corporation hundreds of thousands were  sucked in to a life of dependence  and no work and our city was almost  brought to its knees so when I had a  chance to go to work in the government I  had those two things in my mind success  and civil rights not really success in  poverty and so I joined the Pataki  administration in 1995 luckily right at  the beginning of the welfare reform  movement in America Governor Zangler  governor's Thompson Bill Clinton Newt  Gingrich decided to change welfare as we  know it and I worked there for Governor  Pataki and then for mayor Giuliani for  that entire period and what did we do we  required work in return for public  assistance we made it a clear  expectation that it was a reciprocal  relationship between the receiver of  assistance and the provider of aid in  that regard we changed the names of our  offices they used to be called think of  this the welfare offices in New York  City usually called income maintenance  centers income maintenance centers we  changed them to be job centers we paid  people that tried to help people get  jobs only if they got jobs we paid for  performance we talked about child  support enforcement we said children  need two parents contributing there's a  responsibility for both parents to care  for their children and we created an  environment where if there wasn't a job  or someone couldn't work we said to them  what we need you to be involved in the  schedule and rigor on a day-to-day basis  of something that looks like a job we'll  provide assistance but we need you to be  in a circumstance where you're mirroring  the schedule and rigor of employment so  that's the tough stuff we also rewarded  work so public health insurance for  low-wage workers we were generous with  it  food stamp benefits for working people  we provided it childcare assistance the  Earned Income Tax Credit we promoted  these supports for low-wage workers so  wages could go further we talked about  family we promoted two-parent families  and always always we understood that in  order to succeed at its best  we needed a thriving job producing  economy and we didn't make any  disparagement about dead-end jobs or  low-wage jobs we said we need jobs any  job is a step up  so we promoted hospitality and  restaurants of health care and every  time I saw those economic development  guys in the halls of government I said  make it happen  we need jobs require work reward work  talk about family promote a strong  economy those are the four principles of  success in this endeavor so what  happened where were the statistics so  everyone knows welfare caseloads  plummeted the peak welfare caseload in  New York City was 1.1 million cash  welfare recipients one in seven New  Yorkers was receiving the dole recently  last year before I left we were down a  350,000 out of a city of more than 8  million tremendous decline but that  wasn't our greatest achievement our  greatest achievement was were crates for  never married mothers were crates for  never married mothers the very people  who were on cash welfare in the first  place the very first people who the  program's was originally set up to help  it went from 43 percent to 63 percent 20  percent point increase unprecedented and  remarkable and it happened all across  the country not only in New York City  child poverty in New York City declined  dramatically in the late 90s  up to - up into mm from 43% to 28%  significant decline in child poverty and  what was the most compelling statistic  of all and this is a national statistic  African American child poverty in the  United States dropped from 44% in 1994  to 30% in 2001 now think of that almost  half African American children were in  poverty before welfare reform less than  a third after welfare now since the  early 2000s I have to say I'm not sure  we've kept focused on those report those  core principles we've gotten away from  the require work requirements we've made  rewarding work sometimes replace work so  that some of the benefits we provide  provide disincentives to people being in  work we haven't talked about the  importance of two-parent families enough  and most egregiously we've allowed our  economy to not grow the greatest tragedy  of the anemic recovery we have been  through these last five years is the  damage it's done to poor Americans so  from someone who's been in the business  who's seen it work successfully and seen  it maybe not work so successfully  require work reward work talk about  family promote a strong economy those  are the keys now I have one last point I  want to make and this is an important  one it is a common desire to promise an  outcome to guarantee that we will if we  do this we will guarantee people's  rising safely into the middle class  governments can't promise that  they can't guarantee that people make  that happen  people make that happen if the  environment is created for that to  happen so to promise an outcome of that  kind to guarantee it as if it would as  if government could provide it it's a  false promise and it's a harmful promise  but if we create an environment whereby  people in poverty can move into work and  increase their incomes you know what  they'll do it they did it before they  proved all of the commentators and all  of the prognosticators wrong about what  would happen if we went to a work-based  welfare program they were the heroes of  welfare reform and we need to get back  to that kind of policy to help poor  Americans in the future thank you very  much thanks for watching  we'd like to know what you think about  fighting poverty so tell us in the  comments below  then please to help us spread the word  by sharing this video for more vision  talks click the subscribe button and  visit the website the pursuit of  happiness calm
 

